#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""idp-disk-watch -- the estate's resident reclaim guardian, with the firewall's third leg.

WHY THIS EXISTS. 2026-10-28. The watcher this replaces was a 5-minute tick that ran the FULL
cleaner with a 240s budget: it held the reclaim lock for 240 of every 300 seconds, so every
interactive door that wanted space found the lock held and answered "another run is doing the
work" -- and that run freed 0M, because the phases it reached first (empty caches, a container
engine that is not answering, a walk of other agents' worktrees) yield nothing, while the tier
that actually holds the weight (2.2G of toolchains, tool cache and estate-unused npm globals) was
LAST and never reached inside the budget. The founder: "60s is too long ... needs to be cleaning
up as long as something isn't useful and does break agents workflow" and "more optimal than once
massive cleanup that times out".

WHAT IT IS. A resident process, not a tick:

  * healthy  -> one statvfs, one heartbeat, sleep. Nothing else. The volume is not walked, no
    cache is measured, nothing is deleted. Cost of a healthy cycle: microseconds.
  * under pressure (free < target) -> ONE bounded step of the estate's cleaner, in the cleaner
    INCREMENTAL mode (the caches, the toolchain tier and the estate's own temp scratch), with a
    time budget. Every step is
    idempotent, so a step that did not finish is the next step's work. The guardian never deletes
    anything itself: the cleaner is the only implementation of the reclaim policy, and every
    safety rule (keep-lists, open files, the estate's own runtime naming a thing, the founder's
    running Chrome) lives there.
  * never disruptive -> the step is bounded (STEP_BUDGET_S, 90s), so the reclaim lock is held for
    at most that long, and a door that finds it held is told "another run is doing the work" with
    the measured free space, which is not a failure.

THE THIRD LEG OF THE EPISTEMIC FIREWALL (2026-10-29). The first two legs grade what an AGENT
says (bin/epistemic_firewall.py: a claim needs three independent pieces of evidence) and what it
was TOLD to do (bin/trajectory_lock.py: it may not wander off the ticket). Neither can see a
resident worker: this guardian has no transcript, no plan and no claims to grade -- and the
measured consequence was eight hours of the same nothing. From ~/.estate/disk-watch.log:

    step free=2361M target=2500M rc=3 freed=-2M met=no after=2280M
    step free=2280M target=2500M rc=4 freed=?M met=? after=?M

rc=4 is a step cut short. `freed=?M` is the guardian reporting a field it never measured, because
the child was SIGKILLed before it could print its one RESULT line. The guardian then did exactly
the same thing again, for thirty minutes: 2361 -> 2280 -> 1759 -> 919 -> 437 -> 396 MB. Diligent,
looping, and blind. So this guardian now does three things it did not do before:

  1. IT OWNS THE MEASUREMENT. The freed-bytes and target-met fields are the GUARDIAN's own
     statvfs readings, before and after the step -- not the child's self-report. The child is
     still the only thing that deletes anything, but it is not the only thing that measures, and a
     worker's report of its own success is not evidence. ("When Do Agent Loops Mistake Stagnation
     for Progress?", OpenAlex W7171902361: a frontier agent claimed improvement on every one of
     54 cycles while 56% of those cycles measured zero or below.)
  2. IT TERMINATES BEFORE IT KILLS. The step is cut short with SIGTERM first, so the cleaner's
     EXIT trap runs and prints its RESULT line; SIGKILL is the last resort, after a grace period.
     A step that ends now always ends with a measured line.
  3. IT ESCALATES INSTEAD OF REPEATING. Each step is judged by the shared gate
     (platform/estate/libexec/epistemic_loop.py) against the measured free space. An action that
     measures no progress three times is a LOOP, not work. The guardian then walks a phase ladder
     -- add the worktree tier, then docker and clones -- and when every rung has looped it stops
     stepping and raises a structured incident with an agent invocation, because the answer is no
     longer "try the same thing again".

THE TARGET. The executor refuses to start a job below MIN_FREE_MB (1500 MB, in
mcp/plugins/estate_executor.py), so the guardian's goal is the estate's goal: keep the volume
comfortably above the floor. TARGET_MB (2500) is the working level, so the floor is never a
surprise, and the guardian stops the moment the target is met -- reclaiming past the goal is
disruption nobody asked for.

WHY 2500 AND NOT 4000 (measured 2026-10-28). The target has to be REACHABLE. With 4000 MB
asked for, the measured blocked-by table showed everything left is protected: the node runtime
1571M (the estate's own code names a version inside it), rustup 1341M (its default toolchain),
the tool cache 1542M (the live components are kept), the Go module cache 773M (a build holds it)
and ollama's weights. A target that cannot be met turns the guardian into a perpetual reclaim
that eventually eats the tool cache's last-of-all entry (py312-venv, 654M, a 17-minute cold
rebuild) and still misses the target. 2500 MB is reachable (2011M measured free, plus the ~660M
of regenerable tool-cache weight and the 534M uv cache when no uv process is running) and it
leaves 1000 MB of headroom above the executor's floor. The spine's FAIL threshold stays the
floor itself (1500 MB, in catalog/critical-functions.json): that is the line the estate's doors
break at, and it is the line an agent is woken for.

THE HEARTBEAT. ~/.estate/heartbeats/disk-guardian.json, written every cycle. This is what the
critical-function spine watches: the guardian being ALIVE is one thing, and the volume being
above the floor is another, and the spine checks both -- because a guardian that runs and frees
nothing is exactly the failure this file was written to end.

THE MEMORY BRAKE. The founder, 2026-09-10: "no i killed ollama because machine is slow". A
resident process on an 8GB laptop must not be the reason the machine crawls, so the guardian
measures memory every cycle and stops the one process that causes the grind (ollama) when the
RAM or swap floor is crossed. It touches nothing else, and it says so in the log.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

VOLUME = os.environ.get("DISK_GUARD_PATH", "/System/Volumes/Data")
TARGET_MB = int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_TARGET_MB", "2500"))
CYCLE_S = int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_CYCLE_S", "20"))
STEP_BUDGET_S = int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_STEP_BUDGET_S", "90"))
LOG = os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_LOG", os.path.expanduser("~/.estate/disk-watch.log"))
HEARTBEAT = os.environ.get(
    "DISK_WATCH_HEARTBEAT", os.path.expanduser("~/.estate/heartbeats/disk-guardian.json")
)
CLEANER = os.environ.get(
    "DISK_WATCH_CLEANER", os.path.expanduser("~/.estate/libexec/disk-cleanup.sh")
)
RAM_FLOOR_MB = int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_RAM_FLOOR_MB", "200"))
SWAP_MAX_PCT = int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_SWAP_MAX_PCT", "70"))
BRAKE_PROCS = os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_BRAKE_PROCS", "ollama").split()
# A brake that fires every cycle is a loop, not a brake.
BRAKE_COOLDOWN_S = int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_BRAKE_COOLDOWN_S", "300"))
FAIL_ALERT_EVERY_S = int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_FAIL_ALERT_EVERY_S", "3600"))
# The escalation ladder: what the guardian tries next when the current phase set measurably
# does not work. The base set is the safe one; the worktree tier is where the gigabytes are
# (measured 2026-10-29: 35 registered worktrees, 11.29 GB, of which 4.55 GB is the same
# tracked content materialised 27 times) and it is protected by the cleaner's own claim checks,
# so asking for it is safe -- it is the cleaner's policy that decides what it may delete.
PHASE_LADDER = (
    "caches,critical,temp",
    "caches,critical,temp,worktrees",
    "caches,critical,temp,worktrees,docker,clones",
)
ESCALATION_STATE = Path(
    os.environ.get(
        "DISK_WATCH_ESCALATION_STATE", os.path.expanduser("~/.estate/disk-watch.escalation.json")
    )
)
# How long the guardian waits before it may raise the incident again after a full-ladder
# escalation. The spine's own backoff is separate: this is the guardian's, because the guardian
# is the thing that noticed.
ESCALATION_ALERT_EVERY_S = int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_ESCALATION_ALERT_S", "1800"))

_STOP = False


def _stop(signum, _frame):  # pragma: no cover - signal path
    global _STOP
    _STOP = True


def now_iso() -> str:
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


def log(line: str) -> None:
    """One line, appended. The log is the estate's evidence, so it is never truncated."""
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(LOG), exist_ok=True)
        with open(LOG, "a") as fh:
            fh.write(f"{now_iso()} {line}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass


def free_mb() -> int | None:
    """Free space on the data volume, in MB. None when it cannot be read (BLIND, not zero)."""
    try:
        st = os.statvfs(VOLUME)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return int(st.f_bavail * st.f_frsize / (1024 * 1024))


def _mach_free_ram_mb() -> int | None:
    """Free RAM in MB, read straight from the kernel. None when it cannot be read.

    MEASURED 2026-10-29: shelling out to `vm_stat` cost 0.11 s normally, but 7.6 s on the
    stressed volume where this guardian actually runs -- a spawn, not a measurement, was the
    cost. `host_statistics64` answers in 0.1 ms and spawns nothing, so the brake's input no
    longer depends on the machine being healthy enough to fork.

    This is the same number `vm_stat` reports as "Pages free" plus "Pages speculative".
    """
    try:
        import ctypes

        libc = ctypes.CDLL("/usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib", use_errno=True)
        host = libc.mach_host_self()

        class VMStat(ctypes.Structure):
            _fields_ = [
                (n, ctypes.c_uint32)
                for n in ("free_count", "active_count", "inactive_count", "wire_count")
            ] + [
                (n, ctypes.c_uint64)
                for n in (
                    "zero_fill_count",
                    "reactivations",
                    "pageins",
                    "pageouts",
                    "faults",
                    "cow_faults",
                    "lookups",
                    "hits",
                    "purges",
                )
            ] + [
                ("purgeable_count", ctypes.c_uint32),
                ("speculative_count", ctypes.c_uint32),
            ] + [
                (n, ctypes.c_uint64)
                for n in ("decompressions", "compressions", "swapins", "swapouts")
            ] + [
                ("compressor_page_count", ctypes.c_uint32),
                ("throttled_count", ctypes.c_uint32),
                ("external_page_count", ctypes.c_uint32),
                ("internal_page_count", ctypes.c_uint32),
            ] + [("total_uncompressed_pages_in_compressor", ctypes.c_uint64)]

        stat = VMStat()
        count = ctypes.c_uint32(ctypes.sizeof(VMStat))
        if libc.host_statistics64(host, 4, ctypes.byref(stat), ctypes.byref(count)) != 0:
            return None
        page_size = ctypes.c_uint32()
        libc.host_page_size(host, ctypes.byref(page_size))
        if not page_size.value:
            return None
        return (
            stat.free_count + stat.speculative_count
        ) * page_size.value // (1024 * 1024)
    except Exception:  # ctypes, struct layout, symbol -- any of it means "not measured"
        return None


def _mach_swap_pct() -> int | None:
    """Swap usage as a percentage, read straight from the kernel. None when unavailable.

    `host_statistics64` gives swapins/swapouts (cumulative counters), not current usage, so the
    current figure comes from `sysctl vm.swapusage` -- executed, not read, because on this
    machine the sysctl binary is not a text file.
    """
    sysctl = shutil.which("sysctl") or "/usr/sbin/sysctl"
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            [sysctl, "-n", "vm.swapusage"],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=10,
        ).stdout
        m = re.search(r"used\s*=\s*([\d.]+)[MG]", out)
        t = re.search(r"total\s*=\s*([\d.]+)[MG]", out)
        if m and t and float(t.group(1)) > 0:
            return int(float(m.group(1)) / float(t.group(1)) * 100)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, ValueError):
        pass
    return None


def memory_pressure() -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """(under pressure, measured) -- an unreadable measurement is UNKNOWN, never zero.

    MEASURED 2026-10-29, twice:
      * /usr/bin/vm_stat on this machine is a Mach-O BINARY. Reading the file produced 134 KB of
        Mach-O segments and no "Pages free" line at all, so the RAM reading was ALWAYS None --
        the guardian printed "free=?M" every cycle and braked on swap alone.
      * Executing it fixed the reading but cost a process spawn: 0.11 s when the machine is
        healthy, 7.6 s on the stressed volume where the guardian actually runs. A brake whose
        input costs a fork is a brake that stops working exactly when it is needed.

    So RAM comes from the kernel through `host_statistics64` (0.1 ms, no spawn), with the
    executed `vm_stat` as the fallback for a machine where the mach call does not work. Swap
    comes from `sysctl vm.swapusage`, executed.

    The binaries are resolved through PATH, with the absolute path as the fallback, because a
    measurement the guardian cannot be tested against is a measurement nobody can falsify.
    launchd's PATH is /usr/bin:/bin, so the fallback is what runs in production.
    """
    free_ram = _mach_free_ram_mb()
    swap_pct = _mach_swap_pct()
    if free_ram is None:
        free_ram = _vm_stat_free_ram_mb()
    measured = f"free={free_ram if free_ram is not None else '?'}M RAM swap={swap_pct if swap_pct is not None else '?'}%"
    if free_ram is not None and free_ram < RAM_FLOOR_MB:
        return True, measured
    if swap_pct is not None and swap_pct > SWAP_MAX_PCT:
        return True, measured
    return False, measured


def _vm_stat_free_ram_mb() -> int | None:
    """Free RAM in MB from `vm_stat`, EXECUTED (never read -- it is a binary). None if unreadable."""
    vm_stat = shutil.which("vm_stat") or "/usr/bin/vm_stat"
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [vm_stat],
            capture_output=True,
            timeout=10,
        )
        text = proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace")
        page_size = 4096
        free_ram = None
        for line in text.splitlines():
            if "page size of" in line:
                page_size = int(line.split("page size of")[1].split()[0])
            elif line.startswith("Pages free"):
                free_ram = int(line.split(":")[1].strip().rstrip(".")) * page_size // (1024 * 1024)
            elif line.startswith("Pages speculative"):
                spec = int(line.split(":")[1].strip().rstrip(".")) * page_size // (1024 * 1024)
                if free_ram is not None:
                    free_ram += spec
        return free_ram
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None


_LAST_BRAKE = 0.0


def brake(pressure: bool, measured: str) -> None:
    """Stop the one process that grinds an 8GB laptop. Nothing else is touched.

    The brake refuses to fire unless pressure was MEASURED (measured 2026-10-29: brake() took
    only a string, so any caller -- including one that never checked -- could stop the founder's
    tooling; a brake that is a property of its caller is not a brake). A caller that did not
    measure pressure passes pressure=False and nothing is stopped.


    The brake is a brake, not a loop: measured 2026-10-29, ollama was stopped on EVERY cycle
    (20s apart) while the swap stayed high, because nothing remembered that it had already
    stopped it. Each stop is logged as if it were new, which made the log a lie about how often
    the estate interfered with the founder's tooling. One stop per cooldown, and the log says
    when the next one is allowed.
    """
    global _LAST_BRAKE
    if not pressure:
        return
    now = time.time()
    if now - _LAST_BRAKE < BRAKE_COOLDOWN_S:
        return
    for proc in BRAKE_PROCS:
        try:
            if subprocess.run(
                ["/usr/bin/pgrep", "-x", proc], capture_output=True, timeout=10
            ).returncode == 0:
                subprocess.run(
                    ["/usr/bin/pkill", "-TERM", "-x", proc], capture_output=True, timeout=10
                )
                _LAST_BRAKE = now
                log(
                    f"memory brake: stopped {proc} ({measured}); "
                    f"the next stop is at most {BRAKE_COOLDOWN_S}s away"
                )
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            continue


def parse_result(stdout: str) -> dict:
    """The cleaner's one machine-readable line: RESULT mode=... free_before_mb=... ."""
    out: dict[str, str] = {}
    for line in stdout.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("RESULT "):
            for token in line.split()[1:]:
                if "=" in token:
                    k, v = token.split("=", 1)
                    out[k] = v
    return out


# -- the third leg: the gate the worker cannot write to -----------------------------------------------


def _load_gate():
    """Find platform/estate/libexec/epistemic_loop.py, wherever this guardian was installed.

    Two layouts exist: in a checkout it is ../platform/estate/libexec, and installed it is
    ../libexec (bin/ and libexec/ are siblings under ~/.estate). A guardian that cannot find the
    gate must SAY so rather than silently go back to the behaviour that looped for eight hours.
    """
    here = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
    for cand in (here.parent / "libexec", here.parent / "platform" / "estate" / "libexec"):
        if (cand / "epistemic_loop.py").exists():
            if str(cand) not in sys.path:
                sys.path.insert(0, str(cand))
            try:
                import epistemic_loop  # noqa: PLC0415 -- a deliberate, reported import

                return epistemic_loop
            except ImportError:
                continue
    return None


_GATE = _load_gate()
_GATE_WARNED = False


def gate():
    """The shared gate, or None when it could not be loaded (which is itself reported)."""
    global _GATE_WARNED
    if _GATE is None and not _GATE_WARNED:
        _GATE_WARNED = True
        log(
            "BLIND the epistemic gate (epistemic_loop.py) could not be loaded; the guardian "
            "cannot grade its own steps and will keep stepping without escalation"
        )
    return _GATE


# -- the escalation state ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def load_escalation() -> dict:
    try:
        data = json.loads(ESCALATION_STATE.read_text())
        return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return {}


def save_escalation(state: dict) -> None:
    try:
        ESCALATION_STATE.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        tmp = ESCALATION_STATE.with_suffix(".tmp")
        tmp.write_text(json.dumps(state, indent=1))
        os.replace(tmp, ESCALATION_STATE)
    except OSError:
        pass


# -- the step ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def step(free_before: int, beat=None, phases: str = "") -> tuple[int, dict, str]:
    """ONE bounded step of the estate's cleaner, in its incremental mode.

    Returns (exit code, the parsed RESULT line, the raw output). A step that is cut short is not
    a failure: the cleaner bounds its own deletes and resumes them on the next step.

    `beat` is called while the step runs, so the guardian can keep its heartbeat fresh DURING a
    long step. Measured 2026-10-29: the heartbeat was written only AFTER the step returned, so a
    guardian working correctly for 90s looked dead to the spine (max_age 180s) and the spine
    raised a false incident and woke an agent for a guardian that was doing its job.

    `phases` overrides the cleaner's phase set, which is how the escalation ladder reaches the
    worktree tier. Empty means the cleaner's own incremental default.
    """
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["DISK_CLEANUP_INCREMENTAL"] = "1"
    env["DISK_CLEANUP_TARGET_MB"] = str(TARGET_MB)
    env["DISK_CLEANUP_DELETE_S"] = str(max(10, STEP_BUDGET_S // 3))
    if phases:
        env["DISK_CLEANUP_PHASES"] = phases
    argv = ["/bin/bash", CLEANER, "true", "false", "0", "false", str(STEP_BUDGET_S)]
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(
            argv,
            env=env,
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
            text=True,
        )
    except OSError as exc:
        return 1, {}, f"the cleaner could not be started: {exc}"
    deadline = time.time() + STEP_BUDGET_S + 30
    out = ""
    while True:
        try:
            out, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=15)
            break
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            # Still working: say so, so the spine sees a live guardian.
            if beat is not None:
                beat()
            if time.time() > deadline:
                # TERM FIRST, THEN KILL. Measured 2026-10-29: the guardian used SIGKILL,
                # which the cleaner's trap cannot catch, so the step ended with NO RESULT line
                # and the guardian logged freed=?M -- a field it had never measured. SIGTERM
                # lets the cleaner's EXIT trap print the line it owes; SIGKILL is the last
                # resort, after a grace period.
                try:
                    proc.terminate()
                except OSError:
                    pass
                try:
                    out, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=10)
                except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                    try:
                        proc.kill()
                    except OSError:
                        pass
                    try:
                        out, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=5)
                    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                        out = ""
                return 4, parse_result(out), out
    return proc.returncode, parse_result(out), out


def synth_result(free_before: int, free_after: int | None) -> dict:
    """The guardian's OWN RESULT line, from its own statvfs readings.

    This is the measurement the guardian reports, because the child's self-report is not
    evidence -- and because a step cut short by the door's ceiling produced no line at all, so
    the guardian was printing `?` for a number it could measure for itself.

    When the volume cannot be read at all, the fields say `blind` rather than a number. The
    contract is not "never print a non-numeric field"; it is "never print a number this file did
    not measure". A zero here would be a claim that nothing was freed, which is a different fact
    from "the volume could not be read".
    """
    if free_after is None:
        return {
            "mode": "guardian",
            "free_before_mb": str(free_before),
            "free_after_mb": "blind",
            "target_mb": str(TARGET_MB),
            "met": "blind",
            "freed_mb": "blind",
        }
    return {
        "mode": "guardian",
        "free_before_mb": str(free_before),
        "free_after_mb": str(free_after),
        "target_mb": str(TARGET_MB),
        "met": "yes" if free_after >= TARGET_MB else "no",
        "freed_mb": str(free_after - free_before),
    }


def heartbeat(state: str, free: int | None, extra: dict) -> None:
    """The spine reads this file. It is written every cycle, healthy or not."""
    payload = {
        "at": now_iso(),
        "state": state,
        "free_mb": free,
        "target_mb": TARGET_MB,
        "volume": VOLUME,
        "pid": os.getpid(),
    }
    payload.update(extra)
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(HEARTBEAT), exist_ok=True)
        tmp = HEARTBEAT + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
            json.dump(payload, fh)
        os.replace(tmp, HEARTBEAT)
    except OSError:
        pass


def notify(message: str) -> None:
    """The house alert: a macOS notification. The spine's alerts go to the inbox as well."""
    try:
        subprocess.run(
            [
                "/usr/bin/osascript",
                "-e",
                f'display notification "{message}" with title "Estate disk" sound name "Basso"',
            ],
            capture_output=True,
            timeout=15,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass


def escalate_incident(reason: str, evidence: dict) -> None:
    """The guardian has run out of rungs: raise a structured incident and wake an agent.

    The founder's rule (2026-09-12, AGENTS.md section 3): a safeguard is operational only when
    it decides on real actions and someone is woken to act on the decision. The incident carries
    the measured evidence -- what the ladder was, what each rung freed, why it stopped -- so the
    agent does not have to re-derive what the guardian already proved.
    """
    stamp = datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")
    incident = {
        "id": f"disk-guardian-loop-{stamp}",
        "function": "disk-guardian",
        "kind": "loop",
        "severity": "critical",
        "detected_at": now_iso(),
        "why": reason,
        "evidence": evidence,
    }
    try:
        d = Path(os.path.expanduser("~/.estate/incidents"))
        d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        (d / f"{incident['id']}.json").write_text(json.dumps(incident, indent=1))
        with (d / "ledger.jsonl").open("a") as fh:
            fh.write(json.dumps(incident) + "\n")
    except OSError:
        pass
    try:
        inbox = Path(os.path.expanduser("~/.estate/alerts/inbox.jsonl"))
        inbox.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with inbox.open("a") as fh:
            fh.write(
                json.dumps(
                    {
                        "ts": time.time(),
                        "source": "disk-guardian",
                        "level": "error",
                        "msg": reason,
                    }
                )
                + "\n"
            )
    except OSError:
        pass
    notify("The disk guardian exhausted every phase set without freeing space. See the incident.")
    invoke = Path(os.path.expanduser("~/.estate/libexec/agent-invoke.sh"))
    if invoke.exists():
        payload = {
            "incident": incident,
            "task": (
                "The estate's disk reclaim guardian measured no progress through every phase set "
                "it has (caches, critical, temp, worktrees, docker, clones) and stopped stepping. "
                "Read ~/.estate/incidents/latest.json and the ledger at "
                "~/.estate/loops/progress.jsonl, then find the mass that is actually reclaimable. "
                "Do not repeat a phase set the ledger already shows to have failed, do not delete "
                "another agent's live, dirty or claimed worktree, and do not touch the founder's "
                "running Chrome or live tooling. Report what you measured."
            ),
            "runner": "claude",
            "repo": os.path.expanduser("~/Documents/code/idp"),
            "budget": 20,
        }
        try:
            subprocess.run(
                ["/bin/bash", str(invoke), "--json", json.dumps(payload)],
                capture_output=True,
                text=True,
                timeout=90,
            )
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            pass


def main() -> int:
    if "--print-gate" in sys.argv:
        # The gate's presence is a fact the estate must be able to check from outside the
        # guardian: a guardian that lost its gate must be visible, not silently degraded.
        g = gate()
        if g is None:
            print("BLIND epistemic_loop.py could not be loaded")
            return 2
        print(f"epistemic_loop loaded from {g.__file__}")
        return 0
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, _stop)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, _stop)
    steps = 0
    last_fail_alert = 0.0
    state = load_escalation()
    rung = int(state.get("rung", 0))
    last_escalation_alert = float(state.get("last_alert", 0.0))
    log(
        f"guardian started: target={TARGET_MB}M on {VOLUME}, cycle={CYCLE_S}s, "
        f"step budget={STEP_BUDGET_S}s, cleaner={CLEANER}, "
        f"gate={'epistemic_loop' if gate() else 'BLIND'}"
    )
    while not _STOP:
        free = free_mb()
        pressure, measured = memory_pressure()
        if pressure:
            brake(True, measured)
        if free is None:
            heartbeat("blind", None, {"memory": measured})
            log(f"BLIND cannot read {VOLUME}: the guardian cannot see the volume")
            time.sleep(CYCLE_S)
            continue

        if free >= TARGET_MB:
            # Healthy: nothing is measured, nothing is deleted. This cycle costs one statvfs.
            heartbeat("healthy", free, {"memory": measured, "steps": steps})
            # A rung that produced progress is a rung that worked: fall back to the safe base.
            if rung:
                rung = 0
                state = {"rung": 0, "last_alert": last_escalation_alert}
                save_escalation(state)
                log("the volume is above the target; the escalation ladder resets to its base rung")
            time.sleep(CYCLE_S)
            continue

        # Under pressure: one bounded step. The heartbeat goes out BEFORE the step, and is
        # refreshed while it runs: a guardian that is working is alive, and the spine must never
        # have to guess which of the two it is looking at.
        step_started = now_iso()
        phases = PHASE_LADDER[min(rung, len(PHASE_LADDER) - 1)]

        def beat() -> None:
            heartbeat(
                "reclaiming",
                free,
                {"memory": measured, "steps": steps, "step_started_at": step_started},
            )

        beat()
        rc, result, raw = step(free, beat)
        steps += 1
        after_mb = free_mb()
        # THE GUARDIAN OWNS THE MEASUREMENT. The child's RESULT line is data; the guardian's own
        # statvfs reading is the evidence. When the child was cut short and printed nothing, the
        # guardian still reports a number, because it measured one.
        child_after = result.get("free_after_mb")
        if after_mb is None and child_after and child_after.isdigit():
            # The guardian's own statvfs failed but the child managed to measure the volume
            # before it was cut short. Use the child's measurement and say where it came from.
            after_mb = int(child_after)
            own = synth_result(free, after_mb)
            own["free_after_source"] = "child"
        else:
            own = synth_result(free, after_mb)
            own["free_after_source"] = "guardian"
        if child_after and child_after.isdigit():
            own["child_free_after_mb"] = child_after
        freed = own["freed_mb"]
        met = own["met"]
        after = own["free_after_mb"]

        verdict_row = None
        g = gate()
        if g is not None:
            ledger = g.LoopLedger()
            prog = g.ProgressGate(
                subject="disk-guardian",
                metric="free_mb",
                ledger=ledger,
                no_progress_limit=int(os.environ.get("DISK_WATCH_NO_PROGRESS_LIMIT", "3")),
                direction="higher",
            )
            verdict_row = prog.judge(
                f"cleaner phases={phases} budget={STEP_BUDGET_S}",
                {"free_mb": free},
                {"free_mb": after_mb},
                detail={"rc": rc, "child_result": result, "raw_tail": raw[-400:]},
            )

        heartbeat(
            "reclaiming",
            int(after) if after.isdigit() else free,
            {
                "memory": measured,
                "steps": steps,
                "step_started_at": step_started,
                "last_rc": rc,
                "last_freed_mb": freed,
                "last_met": met,
                "last_result": own,
                "phases": phases,
                "rung": rung,
                "verdict": (verdict_row or {}).get("verdict"),
            },
        )
        log(
            f"step free={free}M target={TARGET_MB}M rc={rc} freed={freed}M met={met} "
            f"after={after}M memory={measured} phases={phases} "
            f"verdict={(verdict_row or {}).get('verdict', 'none')}"
        )

        # THE LOOP BREAKER. An action that measurably does not work is not repeated: the
        # guardian walks the ladder instead, and when every rung has failed it stops stepping
        # and wakes an agent with the evidence. Measured 2026-10-29: the guardian repeated one
        # useless step for thirty minutes while the volume fell from 2361M to 396M.
        verdict = str((verdict_row or {}).get("verdict", ""))
        looped = bool(g is not None and verdict == str(g.LOOP))
        blind = bool(g is not None and verdict == str(g.BLIND))
        if blind:
            # BLIND IS NOT ZERO. The volume could not be measured, so the guardian does not know
            # whether it is making progress -- and it says so instead of quietly stepping on.
            now = time.time()
            if now - last_escalation_alert >= ESCALATION_ALERT_EVERY_S:
                last_escalation_alert = now
                save_escalation({"rung": rung, "last_alert": last_escalation_alert})
                escalate_incident(
                    "the volume could not be measured, so the guardian cannot tell progress "
                    "from stagnation; it stopped stepping rather than stepping blind",
                    {
                        "ladder": list(PHASE_LADDER),
                        "rung": rung,
                        "free_mb": free,
                        "target_mb": TARGET_MB,
                        "last_result": own,
                        "verdict": verdict_row,
                    },
                )
                log("BLIND the volume could not be measured; an incident was raised")
        if looped:
            if rung < len(PHASE_LADDER) - 1:
                rung += 1
                state = {"rung": rung, "last_alert": last_escalation_alert}
                save_escalation(state)
                log(
                    f"LOOP the phase set {phases} measured no progress; escalating to "
                    f"{PHASE_LADDER[rung]} instead of repeating it"
                )
            else:
                now = time.time()
                if now - last_escalation_alert >= ESCALATION_ALERT_EVERY_S:
                    last_escalation_alert = now
                    state = {"rung": rung, "last_alert": last_escalation_alert}
                    save_escalation(state)
                    escalate_incident(
                        "every reclaim phase set measured no progress; the guardian stopped "
                        "stepping rather than repeating a step that does not work",
                        {
                            "ladder": list(PHASE_LADDER),
                            "rung": rung,
                            "free_mb": free,
                            "target_mb": TARGET_MB,
                            "last_result": own,
                            "verdict": verdict_row,
                        },
                    )
                    log("ESCALATED every phase set failed; an incident was raised and an agent woken")

        if rc == 3:
            # A delete that could not be proven: rare, and worth saying out loud.
            now = time.time()
            if now - last_fail_alert >= FAIL_ALERT_EVERY_S:
                notify("Reclaim could not prove a delete (exit 3). See ~/.estate/disk-watch.log")
                last_fail_alert = now
        time.sleep(CYCLE_S)
    log("guardian stopped")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
